messages:
sam: quick check — seeing node streaming clients get a silent disconnect when upstream returns 429 with no body. Should SDKs map this to a RateLimitError (with retry info) or a StreamClosedError?

priya: i'd vote RateLimitError if we can infer 429 / Retry-After. important to surface the original request_id so apps can correlate. best pattern: exception has .request_id and .retry_after fields.

sam: example mapping for node?

lee: here’s a small sketch that we've used in a test harness — treat header-presence first, fall back to stream-close inference:

```
// node pseudo
const res = await http.request(...)
if (res.status === 429) {
  throw new RateLimitError('rate limited', { retryAfter: res.headers['retry-after'], requestId: res.headers['x-request-id'] })
}
// streaming path: no final status body
stream.on('close', () => {
  const hdrs = stream.responseHeaders || {}
  if (hdrs['x-rate-limit'] || hdrs['retry-after']) {
    throw new RateLimitError('stream closed by rate limiter', { retryAfter: hdrs['retry-after'], requestId: hdrs['x-request-id'] })
  }
  throw new StreamClosedError('connection closed unexpectedly', { requestId: hdrs['x-request-id'] })
})
```

priya: +1. also add structured debug logs with request_id as top-level key: e.g. `redwood.request_id=<id>` so traces and logs can be searched easily.

henry: python side — keep same surface. Example exception class we use in other libs:

```
class RateLimitError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, message, *, request_id=None, retry_after=None):
        super().__init__(message)
        self.request_id = request_id
        self.retry_after = retry_after
```

and in client code set logger context: `logger = logging.LoggerAdapter(logger, {'redwood.request_id': req_id})`

sam: how do we get req id reliably for streaming where headers might arrive later?

lee: capture any header you see first and stash in an in-memory metadata object for the stream. if none arrived, generate a correlation id but mark it as client-generated (prefix `cg-`) so servers know it's synthetic. still include it on exceptions/logs.

priya: we should standardize header name across platforms: `x-redwood-request-id`. we currently accept `x-request-id` too but docs should recommend `x-redwood-request-id` for SDKs.

devex-bot: noted — created RFC PR https://github.com/redwood-inference/rfcs/pull/412 to standardize error shapes + request_id header. added checklist: node mapping + python mapping + examples + tests.

sam: thanks — i'll pick up node PR and add tests for stream-close -> RateLimitError with retry_after. will reference PR 412 in commit. :thumbsup:

henry: if you need a test harness, i have a small flaky-rate-limit server you can run locally. ping me.

sam: awesome, will do.
